REGIONAL REGULATION OF TANGGAMUS REGENCY

NUMBER 2 OF 2024

CONCERNING

IMPLEMENTATION OF PROTECTION FOR WOMEN AND CHILDREN

BY THE GRACE OF ALMIGHTY GOD

REGENT OF TANGGAMUS,

Considering:

a. that in order to protect the constitutional rights of women and children in accordance with the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia, the Regional Government is obliged to be responsible for the implementation of protection for women and children against acts of violence;
b. that to meet the indicators for the Implementation of Child-Friendly Regency, concerning protection services, prevention of violence against women and children, regulations are needed to regulate the various roles of regional apparatus institutions and other institutions;
c. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, and letter b, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation concerning the Implementation of Protection for Women and Children;

In View of:

1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number 39 of 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1999 Number 165, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3886);
3. Law Number 1 of 1974 concerning Marriage (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1974 Number 1, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3019), as has been amended several times most recently by Law Number 16 of 2019 Amendment as has been amended several times most recently by Law Number 16 of 2019 (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2019 Number 186, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6401);
4. Law Number 2 of 1997 concerning the Establishment of Level II Regional Regency of Tulang Bawang and Level II Regional Regency of Tanggamus (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1997, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3667);
5. Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Amendment to Law Number 1 of 1974 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2002 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235) as has been amended several times most recently by Law Number 17 of 2016 (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2016 Number 99, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5882);
6. Law Number 13 of 2003 concerning Manpower (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2003 Number 39, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia 4279), as has been amended several times most recently by Law Number 17 of 2016 (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2016 Number 99, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia 5882);
7. Law Number 20 of 2003 concerning National Education System (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2003 Number 78, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia 4301);
8. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);
9. Law Number 13 of 2006 concerning Protection of Witnesses and Victims (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 64, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4635), as has been amended by Law Number 31 of 2014 (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 293, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5602);
10. Law Number 21 of 2007 concerning the Eradication of the Criminal Act of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2007 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4720);
11. Law Number 11 of 2012 concerning the Juvenile Criminal Justice System (SPPA) (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2012 Number 153, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5332);
12. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587), as has been amended several times most recently by Law Number 6 of 2023 (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2023 Number 41, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6856);
13. Law Number 35 of 2014 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 297, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5606);
14. Law Number 12 of 2022 concerning the Criminal Act of Sexual Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2022 Number 120, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6792);
15. Government Regulation Number 4 of 2006 concerning Implementation and Cooperation for Recovery of Victims of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 15, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4604);
16. Government Regulation Number 09 of 2008 concerning Procedures and Mechanisms for Integrated Services for Witnesses/Victims of the Criminal Act of Trafficking in Persons (TPPO) (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2008 Number 22, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4818);
17. Government Regulation Number 70 of 2020 Concerning Procedures for Implementation of Chemical Castration Actions, Installation of Electronic Detection Devices, Rehabilitation, and Announcement of the Identity of Perpetrators of Sexual Violence Against Children (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2020 Number 269, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6585);
18. Government Regulation Number 78 of 2021 concerning Special Protection for Children (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2021 Number 174, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6704);
19. Regulation of the Minister of Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 4 of 2018 concerning Guidelines for the Establishment of Regional Technical Implementation Units for the Protection of Women and Children (State News of the Republic of Indonesia of 2018 Number 532);
20 Regional Regulation of Tanggamus Regency Number 8 of 2016 concerning the Establishment and Composition of Regional Apparatus of Tanggamus Regency (Regional Gazette of Tanggamus Regency of 2016 Number 142, Supplement to the Regional Gazette of Tanggamus Regency Number 47) as has been amended several times most recently by Regional Regulation of Tanggamus Regency Number 9 of 2022 (Regional Gazette of Tanggamus Regency of 2022 Number 212, Supplement to the Regional Gazette of Tanggamus Regency Number 102);
21. Regional Regulation of Tanggamus Regency Number 02 of 2017 concerning Standards for Child Care in Child Social Welfare Institutions (LKSA) (Regional Gazette of Tanggamus Regency of 2017 Number 147);
22. Regional Regulation of Tanggamus Regency Number 1 of 2019 concerning Implementation of Education (Regional Gazette of Tanggamus Regency of 2019 Number 165, Supplement to the Regional Gazette Number 67);
23. Regional Regulation of Tanggamus Regency Number 8 of 2022 concerning Implementation of Child-Friendly Regency (Regional Gazette of Tanggamus Regency of 2022 Number 211, Supplement to the Regional Gazette Number 101);

With Mutual Approval

REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF TANGGAMUS REGENCY

and

REGENT OF TANGGAMUS

HAS DECIDED:

To Establish:

REGIONAL REGULATION OF TANGGAMUS REGENCY CONCERNING IMPLEMENTATION OF PROTECTION FOR WOMEN AND CHILDREN.

CHAPTER II

PURPOSE, OBJECTIVES AND SCOPE

Article 2

The Purpose and Objectives of the protection of women and children from acts of violence are:
a. To prevent all forms of violence against women and children;
b. To provide protection and services to women and children victims of gender-based violence;
c. To provide a sense of security to women and children victims of violence;
d. To restore the physical, psychological and economic condition of women and children victims of violence;
e. The best interests of women and children victims of violence occurring in the domestic and/or public sphere; and
f. To take action against perpetrators of violence against women and children.

CHAPTER III

SCOPE

Article 3

The Scope of this Regional Regulation is as follows:
a. Forms of Violence against Women and children;
b. Rights of Victims of Acts of Violence Against Women and Children;
c. Obligations and Responsibilities;
d. Protection of Women and Children;
e. Cooperation, Partnership and Empowerment;
f. Guidance and Supervision;
g. Child-Friendly Regency (KLA) and Parahita Ekapraya Award (APE)

CHAPTER IV

FORMS OF VIOLENCE AGAINST WOMEN AND CHILDREN

Article 4

Forms of violence against women and children, include:
a. Physical violence;
b. Psychological violence;
c. Sexual violence;
d. Neglect;
e. Exploitation;
f. Other violence, namely:
1\. Victims of pornography;
2\. Victims of disaster and conflict;
3\. Victims of narcotics;
4\. Victims infected with HIV/AIDS;
5\. Victims of terrorism;
6\. Victims of Stigmatization;
7\. Worst Forms of Child Labor (BPTA);
8\. Street children and deviant behavior;
9\. Persons with disabilities and/or;
10\. Children in Need of Special Protection and others.

Article 7

Sexual violence as referred to in Article 4 letter c includes:
a. Acts in the form of sexual harassment;
b. Forced sexual intercourse;
c. Forced unnatural sexual intercourse; and/or
d. Forced sexual intercourse with others for commercial purposes and/or certain purposes.

CHAPTER V

RIGHTS OF VICTIMS OF ACTS OF VIOLENCE AGAINST WOMEN AND CHILDREN

Article 11

Women and children victims of acts of violence receive the following rights:
a. The right to have their dignity respected as human beings;
b. The right to recovery;
c. The right to determine their own decisions;
d. The right to obtain information;
e. The right to confidentiality;
f. The right to social rehabilitation and social reintegration;
g. The right to appropriate, comfortable and needs-based complaint handling;
h. The right of victims and their families to obtain facilitation in the judicial process;
i. The right to assistance; and
j. To receive continuous handling up to the rehabilitation stage.

Article 12

Child victims of acts of violence, in addition to receiving the rights as referred to in Article 11, also receive special rights, as follows:
a. The right to survival, growth, and development;
b. The right to basic population services;
c. The right to equal protection;
d. The right to be free from various stigma; and
e. The right to obtain freedom.

CHAPTER VI

OBLIGATIONS AND RESPONSIBILITIES

Article 13

Obligations and responsibilities in providing protection and Empowerment for women and children, include:
a. Regional Government;
b. Private Sector and Community Institutions;
c. Community;
d. Family and/or parents;
e. Business World; and
f. Mass Media.

Article 14

1. The obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government as referred to in Article 13 letter a, include:
a. Establishing policies for the protection and empowerment of women and children;
b. Implementing policies, protection, and empowerment of women and children from acts of violence in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations;
c. Conducting cooperation in the implementation of protection and empowerment of women and children;
d. Providing support for facilities and infrastructure for the implementation of protection and empowerment of women and children;
e. Allocating budget for the implementation of protection and empowerment of women and children;
f. Guiding and supervising the implementation of protection and empowerment of women and children; and
g. Realizing a Child-Friendly Regency.
2. In order to carry out the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regent together with the DPRD shall establish programs and activities for the protection and empowerment of women and children in the Regional Medium-Term Development Plan (RPJMD).
3. The RPJMD as referred to in paragraph (2) shall also contain the Regional Action Plan (RAD) on the protection of women and children.
4. Further provisions regarding the Regional Action Plan as referred to in paragraph (3), shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 17

The Obligations and Responsibilities of the Business Sector, as referred to in Article 13 letter e include: (1) Company policies with a child and women's perspective in order to provide protection;
2. Products intended for children must be safe for children and/or in accordance with Indonesian National Standards;
3. Contributing to the fulfillment of children's rights through corporate social responsibility.

Article 19

1. To prevent acts of violence against women and children, the Regional Government shall carry out empowerment and awareness to families, parents, children and the community by providing information, guidance and/or counseling, training and realizing a Child-Friendly Regency.
2. In addition to empowerment and awareness as referred to in paragraph (1), the Regional Government shall make efforts as follows:
a. Providing material on the prevention of acts of violence against women and children in formal, non-formal, and informal education;
b. Providing accessibility to obtain education, training, funding, income enhancement and social services;
c. Creating a friendly and safe work environment atmosphere for women;
d. Building community participation and concern for the prevention of protection of women and children from acts of violence;
e. Building and providing an information system that is easily accessible;
f. Building networks and cooperation with law enforcement apparatus, government apparatus, universities and various non-governmental organizations that work and/or care for women and children.

Article 20

1. The prevention of acts of violence against women and children as referred to in Article 19 shall be carried out by Regional Apparatus whose duties and functions are in the fields of:
a. Regional planning and innovation;
b. Social affairs;
c. Health;
d. Education;
e. Manpower;
f. Ministry of Religion;
g. Religious Court;
h. Population and civil registration;
i. Ministry of Law and Human Rights;
j. Women's empowerment and child protection;
k. Family planning;
l. Public Works and Public Housing;
m. Tourism;
n. Cooperatives and micro enterprises;
o. Environment;
p. Village community empowerment;
q. Regional disaster management;
r. Police;
s. Civil service police.
2. The prevention of acts of violence by Regional Apparatus as referred to in paragraph (1), shall be carried out in an integrated and continuous manner based on RPJPD and RPJMD.

Article 21

1. In order to provide protection services to women and children from acts of violence, the Regional Government shall establish a Regional Technical Implementation Unit for the Protection of Women and Children (UPTD PPA), Integrated Referral Service System (SLRT), Violence Against Women/Children Team (Tim KTP/A) at health centers, hospitals, schools, Islamic boarding schools, Women-Friendly and Child-Caring Villages (DRPPA).
2. The Regional Government through UPTD PPA as referred to in paragraph (1), functions to carry out services:
a. Public complaints;
b. Victim outreach;
c. Case management;
d. Temporary shelter;
e. Mediation;
f. Victim assistance; and
g. Victim repatriation.
3. Further provisions regarding the organization and working procedures of UPTD PPA as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be stipulated by Regent Regulation.
4. To assist in the protection of women and children, the Regional Government shall establish:
a. Task Force for the Prevention and Handling of Criminal Acts of Human Trafficking;
b. Task Force for the Prevention of Child Marriage;
c. Task Force for Child-Friendly Regency;
d. Regional Action Committee for the elimination of the worst forms of child labor or Worst Forms of Child Labor (BPTA);
e. Regional Children's Forum (FAD);
f. Community-Based Integrated Child Protection (PATBM) PENA BISMA;
g. Domestic Violence Forum (KDRT);
h. Task Force for the Protection of Women and Children (Satgas PPA);
i. Women-Friendly and Child-Caring Villages (DRPPA);
j. TIM KTP/A at hospitals, health centers, schools, Islamic boarding schools.
5. Further provisions regarding the establishment, duties, and functions of the task force as referred to in paragraph (4) shall be stipulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 22

1. The forms of Regional Government services to victims carried out by UPTD PPA and others as referred to in Article 21 include:
a. Medical services;
b. Medicolegal services (visum);
c. Psychosocial services;
d. Legal services;
e. Economic independence services;
f. Psychological services; and
g. Psychiatric services.
2. Further provisions regarding service mechanisms according to standard operating procedures shall be regulated in a Regent Decree.

Article 25

1. In order to achieve the objectives of protecting women and children, cooperation may be carried out with:
a. Central Government;
b. Provincial Government;
c. Other Regency/City Governments;
d. Non-Governmental Institutions;
e. Community;
f. Universities;
g. Business Sector;
h. Mass Media;
2. Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) includes:
a. Exchange of data and information;
b. Recovery of victims of acts of violence;
c. Repatriation and social reintegration;
d. Provision of evidence and witnesses; and
e. Followed up in accordance with applicable law.
3. Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) and paragraph (2) shall be set forth in the form of a Cooperation Agreement in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.

Article 27

Strengthening and development of Women's Empowerment Service Provider Institutions, is implemented through:
a. capacity building of Women's Empowerment Service Provider Institutions;
b. capacity building of human resources managing Women's Empowerment Service Provider Institutions;
c. assistance in the implementation of Women's Empowerment services;
d. strengthening networks among Women's Empowerment Service Provider Institutions at the district level;
e. facilitation of efforts to fulfill the standards of Women's Empowerment Service Provider Institutions; and
f. development of communication, information, and education about Women's Empowerment services

CHAPTER X

CHILD-FRIENDLY DISTRICT (KLA)

AND PARAHITA EKAPRAYA AWARD (APE)

Article 29

1. To realize a Child-Friendly District (KLA) and Parahita Ekapraya Award (APE) the Regional Government, Private Sector and Community Institutions pay attention to the rights of children and women related to the rights to life, growth and development, protection and participation.
2. The Regional Government, Private Sector and Community Institutions in realizing a Child-Friendly District are incorporated in the Child-Friendly District Task Force and PUG Working Group.
3. The Child-Friendly District Task Force as referred to in paragraph (3) is responsible for the fulfillment of children's rights related to:
a. Civil rights and freedoms;
b. Family environment and alternative care;
c. Basic health and welfare;
d. Education, leisure time utilization and cultural arts activities;
e. Special protection of children;
f. As well as Institutions;
4. The PUG Working Group as referred to in paragraph (2), is responsible for the fulfillment of women's rights with a gender perspective.
5. Provisions related to Child-Friendly District (KLA) and PUG are regulated in Laws and Regulations.

Article 31

1. Funding for protection of women and children victims of violence includes:
a. Medical services, which include doctor examinations, treatment costs, hospital costs, medication costs and medical support costs;
b. Medicolegal services, which include examinations for Visum et Repertum and et Psikiatrikum;
c. Psychosocial services, which consist of counseling and psychological therapy as well as safe houses (shelters);
d. Legal services;
e. Economic strengthening, in the form of services for skills training and providing economic access.
2. Funding management is conducted through technical institutions appointed by the Regional Government which is carried out transparently and can be legally accounted for.
